.TH CCOL_MEMPOOL_CREATE_FROM_PREALLOCATED_BUFFER 3 "2026-08-28" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_mempool_create_from_preallocated_buffer \- create pool from user buffer
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <cmempool.h>

ccol_mempool *ccol_mempool_create_from_preallocated_buffer(
    void *buffer,
    size_t buf_size,
    size_t elem_size,
    bool fallback_to_dynamic_memory,
    bool single_threaded,
    ccol_memmgmt_procs_t *mmgmt_procs,
    char **err);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Creates a memory pool using a user-provided buffer instead of allocating
from the heap. Useful for embedded systems or when heap allocation is
undesirable.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
buffer                     Pointer to preallocated buffer
buf_size                   Size of buffer in bytes
elem_size                  Size of each element in bytes (must be > 0;
                          if smaller than sizeof(uintptr_t) it is rounded
                          up, mirroring ccol_mempool_create(); maximum
                          SIZE_MAX minus the stride rounding)
fallback_to_dynamic_memory If true, allocate from heap when pool exhausted
single_threaded           If true, omit locking
mmgmt_procs               Custom memory management, or NULL
err                       Optional pointer to receive error string
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns pointer to newly created memory pool on success
Returns NULL on failure
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Element count is calculated as buf_size / (stride + 1). An entry carries
  no header; stride is elem_size rounded up to 16 bytes and then, unless
  the library was built with -DCCOL_MEMPOOL_COMPACT_LAYOUT=1, on up to a
  power of two, so every element in the pool is aligned for any object
  type and not just the first. The one extra byte per element is that
  element's own status byte, which lives in the same buffer after the
  entries. CCOL_DECLARE_PREALLOCATED_MEMPOOL_BUFFER sizes its own buffer
  with the same arithmetic and is the way to get it right without
  restating it
Buffer is not freed by ccol_mempool_destroy() (user manages buffer lifetime)
Pool struct itself is still allocated via mmgmt_procs
Returns NULL with error if elem_size is zero
If elem_size is nonzero but < sizeof(uintptr_t), it is rounded up
  (same as ccol_mempool_create())
Returns NULL with error if elem_size would overflow size_t once the
stride rounding is applied
Returns NULL with error if the buffer is not aligned to at least
  16 bytes, which is what every entry the pool hands out is
  aligned to and therefore what entry 0, sitting at the buffer's own
  address, has to satisfy; a buffer declared via
  CCOL_DECLARE_PREALLOCATED_MEMPOOL_BUFFER already meets this
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR CCOL_DECLARE_PREALLOCATED_MEMPOOL_BUFFER (3),
.BR ccol_mempool_create (3),
.BR ccol_mempool_destroy (3)
